{
 "id": "l18",
 "label": "18",
 "title": "Ma- and Maka- verb forms",
 "summary": "Ma- and maka- verbs that show ability, and stressed má- and maká- verbs for accidental actions",
 "minutes": 45,
 "goals": [
  "Understand what the prefixes ma- and maka- add to a verb root",
  "Tell an intransitive ma- verb (active) from a transitive ma- verb (passive)",
  "Form the past, present and future of ma- and maka- verbs",
  "Use maka- to say someone is able to do something",
  "Use stressed má- and maká- for unintentional or accidental actions"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What ma- and maka- do",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Ma-** is a prefix attached to the **beginning of a verb root**. In general, a verb with ma- shows the **capability** to do something."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A ma- verb comes in **two types**, active and passive. Which one you get depends on whether the verb needs an object."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "The verb is…",
      "Meaning",
      "The ma- verb is…",
      "Its subject is…"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "**intransitive**",
       "it does not take an object",
       "**active**",
       "a noun or a nominative pronoun such as [[siyá|pron]] or [[ikáw|pron]], as with -um- and mag- verbs (Lesson 14)"
      ],
      [
       "**transitive**",
       "it needs an object to complete its meaning",
       "**passive**",
       "in the possessive form, such as [[niyá|pron]] or [[mo|pron]], as with -in- verbs (Lesson 15)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Two types of ma- verb"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Maka-** has the same meaning as ma-, except that maka- is **always active**. It takes a noun or a nominative pronoun (such as siyá and ikáw) as its subject."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Not every ma- verb has a maka- partner",
     "items": [
      "A **ma-** verb can be formed from a verb showing **emotion or feeling** (to be angry, to be sad…).",
      "A **maka-** verb can only be formed from a verb showing **action**.",
      "So not all ma- verb forms have an equivalent maka- form."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Building the verbs",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "ma-",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "ma-",
         "r": "other"
        },
        {
         "t": "túlog",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "→ matúlog",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "maka-",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "maka-",
         "r": "other"
        },
        {
         "t": "túlog",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "→ makatúlog",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "to sleep / to be able to sleep"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Intransitive verbs using ma-",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "An intransitive verb with ma- does **not need any other element besides the subject** to form a sentence. Its forms are made like this:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Form",
      "Verb",
      "How it is made",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Root",
       "[[túlog|pred]]",
       "",
       "sleep"
      ],
      [
       "Infinitive",
       "[[matúlog|pred]]",
       "ma- + root",
       "to sleep"
      ],
      [
       "Imperative",
       "[[matúlog|pred]]",
       "ma- + root",
       "(the same form as the infinitive)"
      ],
      [
       "Past",
       "[[natúlog|pred]]",
       "ma- becomes na- + root",
       "slept"
      ],
      [
       "Present",
       "[[natútulog|pred]]",
       "ma- becomes na- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "sleeps/sleep"
      ],
      [
       "Future",
       "[[matútulog|pred]]",
       "ma- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "will sleep"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Ma- with an intransitive verb: túlog"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Two simple swaps",
     "items": [
      "For the **past and present**, ma- changes to **na-**.",
      "For the **present and future**, the **first syllable of the root is repeated**: tú-tulog."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "intrans",
     "title": "Examples of intransitive verbs using ma-"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Transitive verbs using ma-",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A transitive verb needs an object to complete its meaning. Its forms are made in the same way as an intransitive verb, **except that it has no imperative form**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Form",
      "Verb",
      "How it is made",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Root",
       "[[kíta|pred]]",
       "",
       "see"
      ],
      [
       "Infinitive",
       "[[mákita|pred]]",
       "ma- + root",
       "to be seen"
      ],
      [
       "Imperative",
       "none",
       "",
       ""
      ],
      [
       "Past",
       "[[nákíta|pred]]",
       "ma- becomes na- + root",
       "saw"
      ],
      [
       "Present",
       "[[nákikita|pred]]",
       "ma- becomes na- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "sees/see"
      ],
      [
       "Future",
       "[[mákikita|pred]]",
       "ma- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "will see"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Ma- with a transitive verb: kíta",
     "note": "The accents are copied as the book prints this table. In its list of examples below, the book writes the infinitive as makita."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "The subject takes the possessive form",
     "t": "Because a transitive ma- verb is passive, the doer is shown with a possessive pronoun such as **ko**, **mo** or **niyá**: *Nákíta **ko** si Mary* (I saw Mary)."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "trans",
     "title": "Examples of transitive verbs using ma-"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Verbs using maka-",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Generally, adding **maka-** to a verb expresses the **capability to do something** (to be able to). A maka- verb **does not have an imperative form**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Form",
      "Verb",
      "How it is made",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Root",
       "[[túlog|pred]]",
       "",
       "sleep"
      ],
      [
       "Infinitive",
       "[[makatúlog|pred]]",
       "maka- + root",
       "to be able to sleep"
      ],
      [
       "Imperative",
       "none",
       "",
       ""
      ],
      [
       "Past",
       "[[nakatúlog|pred]]",
       "maka- becomes naka- + root",
       "was/were able to sleep"
      ],
      [
       "Present",
       "[[nakatútulog|pred]]",
       "maka- becomes naka- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "is/are able to sleep"
      ],
      [
       "Future",
       "[[makatútulog|pred]]",
       "maka- + duplicated first syllable + root",
       "will be able to sleep"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Maka- with túlog"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The pattern is the same as ma-: **naka-** for the past and present, and the **first syllable of the root repeated** for the present and future."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "maka",
     "title": "Examples of maka- verbs"
    }
   ]
  },
